Use a perfectly flat, uniform #FF00FF background.
No visible grid, borders, labels, numbers, text, ground, or shadows.
Keep the complete character, all tails, and the entire weapon inside each cell,
with generous padding. No clipping and no overlap between cells.

IDENTITY AND SCALE
Preserve Mika's face, fox ears, orange-red hair, braid, ornaments, costume,
armor, colors, tail arrangement, and original glaive design.
Match the clean, detailed chibi rendering of idle-master.png.
Maintain the same three-quarter view facing screen-right in every frame.
Enemies are to screen-right.
Keep identical standing-equivalent anatomical scale, camera distance,
body-root placement, and ground baseline across all six cells.
Allow natural knee compression and torso rotation; do not resize individual poses.

WEAPON AND GRIP
One rigid long-shafted crescent glaive, approximately 1.3 times her standing
height, matching the reference. Preserve shaft length, blade shape,
blade mounting, and the identity of the sharpened edge in every frame.

During the two-handed attack:
Her anatomical RIGHT hand is the upper grip, closer to the blade.
Her anatomical LEFT hand is the lower grip, closer to the butt.
Separate the hands by approximately one shoulder width.
Maintain this hand order throughout the attack.
Keep both hands firmly wrapped around the shaft, with plausible wrists
and softly bent elbows. Never cluster both hands at the shaft midpoint.
No hand swapping, crossed wrists, bent shaft, or detached blade.

The sharpened edge must lead along the cutting arc.
Do not animate the glaive as a spear thrust.
Do not flip or mirror the blade independently between frames.
The shaft may visibly overlap clothing in the foreground, but must never
appear to penetrate her chest, neck, head, or limbs.
The blade must clear her ears, hair, tails, and legs.

ACTION
One compact, powerful diagonal descending cut:
from behind her anatomical right shoulder, with the blade visibly behind
her toward screen-left, sweeping forward and downward toward screen-right.

She attacks in place. The game supplies forward movement.
Use hip rotation, shoulder rotation, controlled weight transfer, and a slight
rear-foot pivot. No running step, jump, spin, or large body translation.

FRAME 1 �?" EXACT RESTING START
Match idle-master.png as closely as possible.
Her right hand holds the glaive upright on the screen-right side of her body.
Her left hand rests on her hip.
Preserve the reference stance, gaze, feet, and relaxed balance.

FRAME 2 �?" CLEAR REAR-SHOULDER WIND-UP
Her left hand has joined the lower shaft; her right hand remains the upper grip.
The hands are clearly separated.
Carry the grip beside the rear side of her torso, outside its silhouette,
rather than bunching both hands in front of her chest.
The shaft is nearly upright, leaning toward upper screen-left.
The crescent blade is high behind her rear shoulder, toward screen-left,
well clear of her head and ears.
Do not lay the shaft diagonally across the center of her chest.
Rotate her hips and shoulders slightly away from the enemy while keeping
her eyes on screen-right.
Both knees are soft; slightly load the rear leg without leaning backward.

FRAME 3 �?" ACCELERATING DIAGONAL CUT
Begin the forward hip turn, followed by shoulders and arms.
Her left hand draws the butt toward her hip while her right hand guides
the upper shaft forward.
Keep the hand spacing and softly bent elbows.
The shaft now rises approximately 40�?"55 degrees toward screen-right.
The blade has traveled from behind the shoulder into the high front-right
part of the same continuous descending cutting arc.
The cutting edge leads downward and forward.
Transfer weight toward the front leg.
This is a rotating cut, not an upward push or spear thrust.

FRAME 4 �?" CONTACT
Show the decisive diagonal cut through the enemy's chest-to-abdomen zone
to screen-right.
The shaft is approximately horizontal to 15 degrees downward toward the blade.
The sharp edge sweeps downward and forward through the contact region;
the weapon is not simply being pushed lengthwise at the enemy.
Her right hand is forward; her left hand stays closer to her body,
controlling the butt. Neither elbow is rigidly locked.
Her hips and shoulders have turned into the strike.
The front knee accepts the weight; the rear foot remains grounded or
pivots lightly with a slightly raised heel.
Keep her torso balanced and her head looking toward the enemy.

FRAME 5 �?" CONTROLLED LOW FOLLOW-THROUGH
Continue the same arc, bringing the blade low toward screen-right.
